  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM EDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nEarly this morning GOES water vapor imagery and model 500mb height \nanalysis showed an upper level ridge remaining in place over the \ncentral U.S. extending north into the Canadian Prairies. Downstream \nof the ridge, weak instability in northwest flow aloft had resulted \nin a few sprinkles over the far western U.P. and passing mid-level \ncloudiness. Elsewhere skies were clear with light winds supporting \nstrong radiational cooling. This has resulted in temperatures\nfalling into the 40s and 50s away from the lakeshores along \nwith patchy dense fog developing. Close to the Great Lakes it \nwas much warmer with temperatures in the low 60s.\n\nAfter some patchy morning fog, a warm and pleasant Labor Day \nWeekend will continue with surface high pressure over Ontario \nextending a ridge south into the U.P. This will keep a weak \npressure gradient and light winds across the U.P. through Labor \nDay. With weak flow and diurnal heating expect a lake breeze to \ndevelop off of the Great Lakes in the afternoons keeping \ntemperatures a bit cooler near the shorelines (but still warm, \nlow 70s). Inland temperatures will warm well into the 70s to \nmid-80s, 10 to 15 degrees above average. Tonight will be a bit \nwarmer than Saturday night as high level cloudiness increases \nwith temperatures falling into the low 50s inland and near 60 \nlakeside. This should limit fog formation with NBM probabilities\ngenerally < 15% for fog development.\n\nAs the upper level ridge shifts east and troughing deepens over\nthe Plains, southerly flow increases cloud cover on Monday and \ncontinues the warming trend for overnight lows. Temps settle \ninto the mid 50s to mid 60s Monday night and upper 50s to 60s \nTuesday night.\n\nPrecip chances return Monday night from the west, continuing into \nTuesday night as the upper level ridge moves east over the Great \nLakes and flattens. This gives way to the deep western trough to \nlift northeast to Manitoba by Tuesday afternoon, which then tracks \neast to Quebec through Wednesday. A resulting sfc low from the \nnorthern Rockies lifts to Manitoba for Tuesday, then moves across \nfar northern Ontario to Quebec through Wednesday. Showers return to \nthe UP ahead of the associated cold front, mainly on Tuesday. \nInstability struggling to reach 500 j/kg mean strong to severe \nstorms are not expected. Widespread rainfall amounts have trended \ndown as deterministic solutions are indicating QPF splits somewhat \naround the UP. Probabilities of at least 1 inch of rain in 24 hours \nare now 30-60% only over the north half and far western Upper \nPeninsula, quickly falling to 10-25% elsewhere. This system also \nyields breezy winds. Initially expecting south winds on Tuesday \nahead of the cold front gusting between 15-30 mph, particulary in \nideal southerly downslope flow. Behind the front, pressure rises and \nCAA support stronger westerly winds on Wednesday with frequent gusts \nof 20-30 mph. Probabilities for gusts exceeding 40 mph are between \n25-50% from Marquette to Whitefish point and Saxon Harbor to \nOntonagon. Could see a Wind Advisory being needed in the Keweenaw \nwhere probabilities for gusts exceeding 45 mph already are between \n50-70%. \n\nWeak high pressure ridging returning to the area bring back much \ncalmer conditions on Thursday. The drying trend in the forecast has \nalso removed lingering precip chances until the weekend, although \nthe marginally cold enough 850 mb temps could yield scattered lake \neffect cloud cover late Wednesday night into Thursday. Otherwise \ntemps return near normal in the upper 60s to mid 70s late next week.",
